  <p class="lead">A Markdown file can be technically readable and still be awkward to publish. A heading may be missing a space, a list may be uneven, a code block may have no language label, or an image may have empty alt text. Some of these problems are mechanical; others require the author to decide what the document is trying to say.</p>

  <p class="lead">That distinction is the practical difference between an <strong>online Markdown formatter</strong> and a <strong>Markdown linter</strong>. A formatter normalizes predictable syntax. A linter finds potential style, structure, and quality problems. Good publishing workflows use both, but they do not give both tools the same permission.</p>

  <div class="answer-card" id="quick-answer">
    <strong>The short answer:</strong> Use a formatter to review narrow, deterministic source changes such as heading spacing and extra blank lines. Use a linter to surface questions about hierarchy, code labels, images, and links. Use both when you want a clean document without giving up editorial control.
  </div>

